A||||11||loafer87gt||What sort of HP gains from head porting?||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||02-07-2001||12:48 AM||||Now that spring is approaching I'm trying to make my final decision on what to with my blown engine on my 87GT. After finding out that GM wants $2340 (including taxes) for a rebuilt 2.8 longblock, and the cheapest engine rebuilder's estimate was over the $2000 mark, I found that one of the rebuilt engines from RSM seems to be the best deal. After talking to Zsolt and Nelson at the shop there, they said that they could put together a 3.0 V6 with a more aggressive cam, 1.6 ration roller rockers, larger throttle body for less than the stock GM longblock. I am having trouble deciding whether I should pay the $400 bucks Canadian to have the Stage III head porting done while I am getting the engine done. Has anyone done the heads on their car and dynoed it to see if there was any noticeable gains? Also, is there anything that you guys would recommend doing differently to the engine?||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||02-07-2001||08:23 AM||||Just a thought.... I've got a new GT with a blown motor, and I've considered a couple options.  If you're not hell-bent on having a "new" motor, you could spend 250 bucks on an iron head mini-van 3.1 V6...  Throw new rings and bearings in it and stuff a cam in...  Lots of possibility.||||reg||1
Z||000002||lou_dias||02-07-2001||08:30 AM||||If you are going to do a standard rebuild, headporting will cause you to lose a ft*lbs or 2 down low and gain 1 or 2 hp up top.  To really see it's benefits, you should get the 1.6 roller rockers and port the intake as well and remove the extra casting from your exhaust manifolds.<BR>You do realize that instead of getting another 2.8 block, you can just rebuild yours into a 3.2 just by changing the crank and pistons (which you were going to do anyway on a rebuild for the same $$$).<BR>All together, you should see and extra 30-40hp and 200lbs of torque.  Don't forget to get everything balanced.||||reg||1
Z||000003||loafer87gt||02-07-2001||11:07 AM||||What year 3.1 has the iron heads on it? Also, what sort of compression ration does it run, and what is the rated HP and torque for that motor? I shouldn't have much of a problem tracking one of those down...||||reg||1
Z||000004||lou_dias||02-07-2001||12:52 PM||||The 89-92 camaro and Firebird's MIGHT have had them, but who cares, just use the Fiero heads or rebuild the 2.8 block you already have and just use 3.1 pistons (with the appropriate overbore for your rebuild...ie. a block bored .030" over means you get 3.1 dished pistons .030" over as well, rings too<BR>) and crank.  That's the only difference.||||reg||1
Z||000005||GT Bastard||02-07-2001||12:56 PM||||Look for a 91 Lumia APV minivan... I know there's plenty others, but I'm not positive which ones and what years.  Factory is 120hp and 185lbs of torque if I remember correctly. But that's with TBI injection. Less hp, more torque.  But slap the Fiero fuel injection on it and you'll have a respectible machine.  It's a noticeable difference.  Fierogod used the same motor in his SE and it does very well!  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Black88GT||02-07-2001||03:51 PM||||Lou, how much was it to have the heads ported?  I have my old 2.8 heads that I am thinking about getting fully rebuilt and ported.  Using si valves and 1.6 ratio roller rockers of course.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||ThatRickGuy||02-07-2001||04:58 PM||||I had my 2.8 rebuilt w/ a 3.1 crank and bored .03 over assembled and painted and resurfaced rods, balanced etc... was right arround $2000 I think after lab fees and random non-engine parts it was about a $2400 jobs, me and a buddy did all the pull/put labor ourselves.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Shaun41178||02-07-2001||06:43 PM||||hp gains from a good port can give as much as 30 hp on a 2.8/3.1/3.4.<P>Really good bowl work, gasket matching and a 5 angle valve job will get you at least 20 hp.  I say at least.  Travis put in SI vlaves and without even a vlave grind or lap he hit the dyno and picked up 14 hp.  With a port and bowl work 20-30 is not far fetched.  hope that answers your question.<P>oh yea price will be between $300 and $1000 depending on how far you want to go.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||loafer87gt||02-07-2001||08:10 PM||||Wow, those are some impressive numbers. How much can a guy expect to pay for a good set of SI valves. Also, what the heck does the SI stand for? I thought valves were made of aluminum.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Shaun41178||02-07-2001||10:12 PM||||SI valves is the name of the company that supplies them.  They are stainless steel.  <P>I know the Fiero Store sells the Si valves for like $170 I think.<P>I have a set of performance heads that I have been trying to get rid of for some time now.  They have Si Valves, all Gm valvetrain components as on the 3.4 crate motor, positive valve seals, spring height measured and same for each spring to give same tension on each spring, 5 angle valve job with back cut, gasket matched by me although it ain't the greatest it is better then nothing and the bowls were opened up and ported to help low lift flow and top end power.  Its not a high dollar bowl porting job but it looks better and flows more then the stockers would.<P>I want $560 for the pair plus shipping.  Expensive yes but will give great power gains.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||lou_dias||02-08-2001||09:42 AM||||I got my ported heads and ported intake from Joe Wynmann at Associated Auto.  I forget the price, it was like 4 years ago I think.  Since then, they've been on 2 other engines.<BR>I showed them to a machine shop that does oem rebuilds and they thought he did a nice job and years later, I showed them to a high-performance machine shop and they said "That's not a port job, this is a port job" and they showed me these v8 heads that were fully ported.  Way too much for the street.  They were ported to make peak power at 8000 rpms...  Way too much for our street machines.  Keep that in mind when you get your heads done.  You shouldn't try to get more than 20 hp out of porting, otherwise it will be out of the useable RPM range of our engines.||||reg||1
